.TH "SKARN-INVENTORY" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.38.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-inventory \- Inventory assistants, MCP servers, guard mode. Free, no
license
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBinventory\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print one inventory record for this machine: per assistant, the completeness
of its session and configuration observation, its declared session store
roots, the client versions observed in a session, its declared and called MCP
server counts, its last activity and session count; per machine, the
configured and effective guard mode. Inventory needs no license for a live
home. With \-\-evidence\-home <dir> (Pro), the record describes the copied
home's sessions and home\-layer configuration. The window defaults to all
history, and \-\-project filters sessions only.
.PP
Print one inventory record for this machine: per assistant, how completely
Skarn could observe its sessions and its configuration, the declared session
store roots, the client versions observed in a session and when, how many MCP
servers its configuration declares by transport and how many its sessions
called in the window, the last activity and the session count; per machine,
the guard mode the configured hooks ask for and the mode the guard runs in. On
a live home, inventory reads the session stores, the assistant configuration
files skarn vet reads, the guard configuration layers and the installed
license, all read\-only and offline. A path is written as an anchor such as
~/.claude.json or $COPILOT_HOME/mcp\-config.json, never as its expanded value,
and a user\-named hook file, plugin directory or custom\-tool root as
<custom>. VS Code's user settings file is listed under copilot as
$VSCODE_APPDATA/Code/User/settings.json when VSCODE_APPDATA is set and not
empty, and otherwise as ~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/settings.json
on macOS, $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Code/User/settings.json or
~/.config/Code/User/settings.json on Linux, and
$APPDATA/Code/User/settings.json or
$USERPROFILE/AppData/Roaming/Code/User/settings.json on Windows; each VS Code
profile's settings file counts in copilot's <custom> row, and a project's
\&.vscode/settings.json is listed in a \-\-project run. The copies of Cline's
MCP settings that the Cline extension left in VS Code's and Cursor's user
directories are listed under cline in the same form, ending in
globalStorage/saoudrizwan.claude\-dev/settings/cline_mcp_settings.json, with
Cursor in place of Code for Cursor's copy. A run with no \-\-project writes no
server name and no project name; with \-\-project, the names of servers called
in sessions in scope and of servers declared in the project\-local
configuration of a project root those sessions recorded are written masked.
Versions are observed in a session, never installed, and each is written as
the session recorded it with no masking, so a detected credential that is
byte\-identical to a recorded version string prints there. The record is the
same object skarn check \-\-format ndjson writes as its skarn.inventory line,
documented in NDJSON\-CONTRACT.md. Inventory of a live home is free and needs
no license. It returns exit 0 on success, 1 on a usage error, 6 when the home
directory cannot be resolved, the \-\-rules pack cannot be loaded or the
collection fails. If collection fails, inventory writes a record with
event.outcome failure and an error.code to stdout. With \-\-evidence\-home
<dir> (Pro), inventory reads the copied home's session stores and home\-layer
configuration. The \-\-hours window defaults to all history. With \-\-project,
stderr says `\-\-evidence\-home: the inventory read no project configuration
file; \-\-project filtered the sessions only`. Each root uses its default
token, such as ~/.grok. The record omits skarn.guard_mode,
skarn.guard_effective and skarn.scope.project_roots_truncated. The host name,
skarn.identity, skarn.org and skarn.scanner_user identify the examiner's
machine. Inventory's configuration discovery follows the same rules as vet for
operator\-named network roots and links below an evidence home or network
root; its session counts come from the session scan, which follows no link
below an evidence home and no link to a network path anywhere, and counts each
refused link as an unreadable store. After the record, inventory prints the
same per\-reason stderr lines for refused links as check; stdout and the
record are unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. json prints the record indented; ndjson prints it as one
skarn.inventory line. One of: json, ndjson. The default is json.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIN\fR
Read sessions modified in the last N hours; 0 reads the whole history. The
default is 720.
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fItool\fR
Read sessions of one tool only, named as check names it; the other assistants
with a session reader are reported as filtered, and their configuration is
still read and counted. A name that matches no session source on this machine
is a usage error.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIlist\fR
Scope to sessions whose project name or root matches an entry of this
comma\-separated list, as check matches it, and write the server and project
names that scope allows.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIlist\fR
Keep only sessions whose recorded user id or email matches an entry of this
comma\-separated list, as check matches it.
.TP
\fB\-\-rules\fR \fIpath\fR
Mask names with this detection rule pack, applied to session\-derived and
configuration\-derived names alike; a missing or malformed pack exits 6.
.TP
\fB\-\-evidence\-home\fR \fIdir\fR
Read a copied home's default session stores (Pro). For configuration
discovery, Skarn reads the copied home's own assistant configuration. Skarn
looks where this machine's operating system keeps each store, so a copy taken
on another operating system can show fewer session or configuration rows. The
Compliance API export root resolves under dir too, and reading it still needs
Enterprise. Session discovery ignores store overrides, XDG_DATA_HOME,
XDG_CONFIG_HOME and SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR. The license, EULA record,
~/.skarn.json, caches and outputs stay with the examiner. Skarn ignores the
copied .skarn.json. Skarn resolves ~/ in sessions_path under dir and reports
skipped absolute paths. All history is in scope unless you specify \-\-hours,
\-\-since or \-\-until. Only an explicit \-\-baseline applies. Assess reads
the copied home's saved permission rules for saved_approvals when in scope.
Vet reads only home\-layer configuration and refuses \-\-all\-projects and
\-\-project\-only. Inventory reads sessions and home\-layer configuration. Its
\-\-project filter applies only to sessions. Check writes the same
skarn.inventory line with \-\-format ndjson and refuses \-\-drop,
\-\-audit\-verify and \-\-baseline\-merge. Restore omits cd and names the
evidence machine's unchecked directory on stderr. The session scan skips
transcript path checks, reads derived metadata only under dir and follows no
link below the evidence home: each refused link counts once as an unreadable
store, and one stderr line per reason gives the count. Configuration discovery
in vet and inventory follows no symbolic link below dir either. Both accept a
plain UNC share as the evidence home and exit 6 for a refused network or
device spelling or a control character. Skarn refuses outputs under dir and
homes that contain the examiner's home or overlap Skarn's config or cache
directories (exit 1). Missing, unreadable or non\-directory paths, paths
containing *, and paths that pass through a link to a network path, a link
Skarn cannot resolve or a component Skarn cannot check for links cause exit 6;
the stderr line names the link. Licensing failures exit 7 (no usable license)
or 5 (insufficient tier) before evidence access. Input files can reside
anywhere. JSON, SARIF, NDJSON and the audit record have no dedicated
evidence\-home field. Finding paths show the directory. The evidence pack
names it on one line. For check, \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error fails incomplete
scans. Requires a Pro license.
